---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import YamlJsonConverter from '../../components/YamlJsonConverter.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="YAML to JSON Converter" description="Convert between YAML and JSON in both directions, entirely in your browser.">
  <YamlJsonConverter client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why YAML and JSON are more compatible than XML and JSON</h2>
  <p>YAML is essentially a superset of JSON's data model (though not its exact syntax) — both represent the same core structures (objects, arrays, scalars), making conversion between them much more reliable than converting from a fundamentally different format like XML.</p>

  <h2>How to use this tool</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Paste YAML or JSON content.</li>
    <li>Get the converted equivalent instantly.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common YAML/JSON Conversion Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Forgetting YAML is whitespace-sensitive</strong> — inconsistent indentation is the most common source of YAML parsing errors when editing converted output.</li>
    <li><strong>Losing YAML-specific features when converting to JSON</strong>, like comments or anchors/aliases, which JSON doesn't support at all.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This YAML to JSON Converter</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Converting a Kubernetes or CI/CD YAML config to JSON for programmatic processing</li>
    <li>Converting a JSON config file to more human-readable YAML</li>
    <li>Debugging a YAML file's actual parsed structure by viewing it as JSON</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>YAML to JSON FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does this preserve YAML comments when converting to JSON?</strong> No — JSON has no comment syntax, so any YAML comments are necessarily dropped in conversion.</p>
</ToolLayout>
